      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
Your house has a few locations where excess air leakage exists:

** Along the bottom of the exterior entrance doors to your house - especially the one in the front of the house.

** The attic hatch can be sealed with a removeable caulking, such as Draft Attack.

** The old HRV ventilation system as well as the bathroom fan that seems to have a broken butterfly damper inside.

** The intentional holes associated with the boiler mechanical room - the chimney &amp; the 2 x combustion air supply pipes in there). There is a 6" combustion air pipe and another 4" hole in the floor (perhaps a drain ?), however they are both available to bring outside air into this room for boiler combustion. You should check with your heating/plumbing contractor to see if there is a way to reduce the size of the floor drain, and perhaps see if the door to the mechanical room can be weather-stripped to prevent cold air from floating out from under the door &amp; across the kitchen floor.

Savings:
If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 145 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.4 GJ, and qualify for a $ 500 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program
</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
The exterior walls of your house are 2x6@16" with R20 insulation. As most new housing has a minimum of R30 walls, I thought I would model adding another 2" (R10) of rigid extruded polystyrene foam board insulation to the exterior of the house ........ including the estimated energy savings &amp; possible grants/rebates to help you decide.

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover some of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding.

Savings:
By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 434 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 10.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

PLEASE NOTE: the DHERP is scheduled to end on January 31, 2024, so you would only have this summer to complete this upgrade &amp; qualify for the $3,000. Completing a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade Please let us know ASAP if you plan to do this, so we can pre-approve you for funding.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <windows>
If you complete an exterior wall insulation and siding upgrade, this is a good time to upgrade the 6 windows in this house with new ENERGY STAR® units. If you proceed, I recommend you install ENERGY STAR® qualified, full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m2·K. ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient windows would have a U-Value rating of no higher than 1.05 W/m2·K.

Savings:
By replacing these 6 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 188 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 250 per window in rebates ($1,500 for the 6 windows) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program
</windows>
                <doors>
Your house has 2 exterior entrance doors and they both have some air leakage around them or along the bottom door sweep. You may be able to have new weather-stripping installed this summer, or perhaps you may choose to replace them with new ENERGY STAR® qualified units. If you replace them, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.

Savings:
By replacing these 2 entry door systems with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 9 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 0.2 GJ. There are currently no eligible rebates available for upgrading exterior doors in this rental house.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem>
VENTILATION - HEAT RECOVERY VENTILATION (HRV)

Your house currently has a very old version of a Heat Recovery Ventilation unit - a Nutone AE-200C. It appears to be operated from switches in the 2 x bathrooms and draws exhaust from both bathrooms and the kitchen, while supplying fresh air to the central hallway. All of the ductwork for this unit runs through the cold attic space and the air inlet pipe on the side wall of the house was frosted shut at the time of my evaluation.

Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality. It doesn't sound like the tenant is even using this ventilation unit and there does not appear to be any signs of excess moisture within the house, so perhaps it is not required ?

You should have it checked out to see if this house requires an HRV or if making the following changes would suffice:
1. Have the kitchen range hood fan vented to the outside.
2. Fix the central bathroom fan so it is working and vented to the outside &amp; install a timer switch.

If your review with a heating &amp; ventilation contractor indicates that an HRV is indeed required, I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system, with a minimum 75% efficiency, designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.

Savings: By installing an ENERGY STAR® rated HRV, with a minimum 75% efficiency, you could potentially save $ 99 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 2.3 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with a very old 82.1% efficient propane furnace. I have modeled an option for you to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 96% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 783 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler that is a minimum 97% AFUE. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
Renewable Energy Systems

The Arctic Energy Alliance administers the Alternative Energy Technologies Program (AETP) that includes rebates for Solar PV systems, including integrated battery energy storage for NWT residents who are not connected to the hydro-electric grid. 

Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the rear facing roof (Southeast), directly connected to the Norman Wells grid (ie: not including a battery storage system). This is estimated to produce about 3,273 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,026, and qualifying for up to $20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program.

For more information and pre-approval rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
https://aea.nt.ca/program/renewable-energy/

NOTE: The GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Please note that only the principal residence of the homeowner is eligible to participate in the Canada Greener Homes Grant program. As this property is a rental unit, it will not be eligible.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DEEP HOME ENERGY RETROFIT PROGRAM

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA.

PLEASE NOTE: The DHERP is scheduled to end on January 31, 2024, so you would only have this year to complete the required upgrades &amp; have us back for a post-retrofit energy evaluation. Please let us know ASAP if you plan to do this, so we can pre-approve you for funding.

For more information, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Norman Wells, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (just the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 2.6 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current oil boiler is chimney vented (with combustion air supply), and can be susceptible to having combustion products back-drafted into the house.

During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors, but they are very old. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following ventilation:
        - Kitchen range hood exhaust fan (does not vent to the outside)
        - Bathroom exhaust fan (was not working at the time of my visit)
        - Clothes dryer - vented to the outside
        - A very old Nutone AE-200C Heat Exchange central ventilation system

If you choose to replace or install or replace any exhaust fans, please select ENERGY STAR® qualified units. For a new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Norman Wells

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Norman Wells:
Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.54 per kWh thereafter
Oil  - $2.17 per litre 
Wood - $100 per cord self-harvested, $800 per cord purchased
Pellets - $10.00 per bag, $660.00 per tonne bulk delivered

